WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.340
A meter has to keep measuring, and keep its record,

2
00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.180
even when the mains is gone.

3
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:11.820
SKU-2 puts measurement, security and an always-on clock on one die.

4
00:00:11.824 --> 00:00:15.224
Voltage and current flow into six 24-bit converters.

5
00:00:16.264 --> 00:00:19.424
They set billing accuracy, so they are dedicated hardware,

6
00:00:19.424 --> 00:00:21.564
calibrated once.

7
00:00:21.564 --> 00:00:24.777
A decimator filters each raw bit stream into clean samples,

8
00:00:24.777 --> 00:00:27.797
so the processor never touches the measurement chain.

9
00:00:27.957 --> 00:00:31.697
What the meter charges for is computed right beside the converters.

10
00:00:33.157 --> 00:00:38.357
Real, reactive and apparent power, and harmonics up to the 15th.

11
00:00:38.453 --> 00:00:41.393
Results cross the bus to be stored and signed.

12
00:00:42.413 --> 00:00:45.953
Secure boot and tamper key wiping protect the code,

13
00:00:45.953 --> 00:00:49.493
and the keys, every reading depends on.

14
00:00:49.511 --> 00:00:52.471
Meter readings go out on the meter's own protocol,

15
00:00:52.471 --> 00:00:56.091
beside the display, the relay and the LEDs.

16
00:00:56.251 --> 00:00:59.231
One die covers the whole meter.

17
00:00:59.231 --> 00:01:03.031
An always-on domain keeps the clock and the tamper log alive on

18
00:01:03.031 --> 00:01:06.311
a backup cell, drawing under 2 microwatts.

19
00:01:06.451 --> 00:01:08.191
SKU-2 is pre-silicon.

20
00:01:08.191 --> 00:01:10.371
Every figure is a target.

21
00:01:10.371 --> 00:01:14.951
Accuracy over a fifteen-year life, and leakage through a thirty-day outage,

22
00:01:14.951 --> 00:01:20.531
must still be proven Bring us your accuracy class and your outage

23
00:01:20.531 --> 00:01:20.831
budget.

24
00:01:20.831 --> 00:01:24.351
We will tell you what SKU-2 must prove.
